## Table 2 — Passing a 648-byte struct: value versus const reference

Times are in nanoseconds per call, using 1,000 samples with 2,000 calls per sample.

| Variant | p50 | p99 | p99.9 | Mean |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| `sum_by_value(Big)` | 27.390 | 58.785 | 157.909 | 29.297 |
| `sum_by_cref(const Big&)` | 13.789 | 37.155 | 73.004 | 14.747 |

- Reported p50 value/reference ratio: **1.99×**.
- Correctness: both functions return **7191.0**, an equal, nonzero result.
- Reported checksum: **30202200000.0**.

Passing by value gives the function its own object, whereas passing by const reference provides access to the existing object without requiring a parameter copy. In this run, the median time for the value version is approximately twice that of the reference version. The result is consistent with additional copying overhead, although generated machine code would be needed to establish exactly how the compiler implements each call. Const references are useful for reading larger objects while avoiding unnecessary copies.


